          %h1#forcing Forcing
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#an_analogy_to_polynomial_rings"} An analogy to polynomial rings
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesInTermsOfClassifyingToposes"} In terms of classifying toposes
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            %strong forcing
            is a way of “adjoining indeterminate objects” to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            in order to make certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/false"} false
            in a resulting new model.
          %p
            The language of forcing is generally used in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            it is more or less equivalent to the construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+sheaves"} categories of sheaves
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            is a “category of sets” that is “forced” to contain a generic model of the theory.
          %h2#an_analogy_to_polynomial_rings An analogy to polynomial rings
          %p
            There are three ways in which to describe the construction of forcing, which can be explained by analogy to a situation in algebra. Suppose that we have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            and we want to know what
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            would be like if it had a nilsquare element, i.e. an
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            such that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^2=0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %em actually
            has any nilsquare elements. There are several things we could do.
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                We could consider some larger ring
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                which contains
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                as a subring, and also contains some nilsquare element
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                generated by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                and that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                is nilsquare.
            %li
              %p
                We could work formally in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                with an extra variable
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                and the axiom that
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mn 2
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^2=0
            %li
              %p
                We could construct the polynomial ring
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R[x]
                and quotient by the ideal
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x^2)
                %em universal
                ring
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R[x]/(x^2)
                containing
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                and also a nilsquare element.
          %p
            Obviously these are more or less equivalent ways of doing the same thing. Arguably, most modern mathematicians would find the third the most natural. It is certainly the most
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category-theoretic
            and the most in line with the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
          %p
            Now suppose instead that we have a model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZF"} ZF
            set theory (for instance), and we want to know what
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            would be like if it contained a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            satisfying some particular properties. We can likewise take three approaches.
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                We can assume that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                sits inside some larger model of ZF, in such a way that there is a set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                outside of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                and the “model generated by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                and
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V[G]
                satisfies the desired properties. The
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                is called a
                %em generic
                set for the desired “notion of forcing.” There is an extra wrinkle in this approach in that such a
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                will not generally exist unless we assume that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                is
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L%C3%B6wenheim-Skolem+theorem"} Löwenheim-Skolem theorem
                guarantees that if ZF is consistent, then it has a countable model.
            %li
              %p
                We can define a new notion of “truth” for statements about
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"} truth values
                but in some more complicated
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebra"} Boolean algebra
                (or
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                with the desired properties” is “true.” This is called
                = succeed "." do
                  %em forcing semantics
            %li
              %p
                We can construct a new model of set theory out of whole cloth from
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %em Boolean valued model
                (or a
                %em Heyting valued model
                if it is
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} intuitionistic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+of+sheaves"} topos of sheaves
                on a suitable
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %em universal
                over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                such that it contains an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                satisfying the desired properties in its
                = succeed "\u2014in" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
                of such a
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesInTermsOfClassifyingToposes"} References – In terms of classifying toposes
                for more.
          %p
            Although by analogy, it would seem that a modern perspective should prefer the third approach (
            %a{:href => "#Shulman20"}> Shulman 20
            ), most material set theorists still seem to prefer one of the first two.
          %p
            Before
            %a{:href => "#Cohen1963a"} Cohen 1963
            showed how forcing could give rise to models of ZF(C),
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Abraham+Fraenkel"} Fraenkel
            introduced the method of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutation+models"} permutation models
            (later refined by Mostowski and Specker), which gave models of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFA"} ZFA
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/urelements"} atoms
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basic+Fraenkel+model"} basic Fraenkel model
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kripke-Joyal+semantics"} Kripke-Joyal semantics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation+topology"} double negation topology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory+FAQ"}> homotopy type theory FAQ
                –
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory+FAQ"} What is homotopy type theory? – For set theorists
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p Exposition and introduction:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Timothy+Chow"}> Timothy Chow
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A beginner’s guide to forcing
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0712.1320"} arXiv:0712.1320
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Schoenfield71"} Schoenfield 71
          %p
            The original proof via forcing of the independence from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+hypothesis"} continuum hypothesis
          %ul
            %li#Cohen1963a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+J.+Cohen"}> Paul J. Cohen
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The independence of the Continuum Hypothesis
                %strong 50
                6 (1963) 1143-1148 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.50.6.1143"}> doi:10.1073/pnas.50.6.1143
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC221287/pdf/pnas00240-0135.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Cohen1963b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+J.+Cohen"}> Paul J. Cohen
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The independence of the Continuum Hypothesis II
                %strong 51
                1 (1963) 105-110 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.51.1.105"}> doi:10.1073/pnas.51.1.105
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC300611/pdf/pnas00175-0117.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Cohen1966
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+J.+Cohen"}> Paul J. Cohen
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Set theory and the continuum hypothesis
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://archive.org/details/settheorycontinu0000cohe"} ark:/13960/s2qndx0d4n7
          %p Historical reminiscences:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+J.+Cohen"}> Paul J. Cohen
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Discovery of Forcing
              %strong 32
              4 (2002) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.rmjm/1181070010"}> euclid.rmjm/1181070010
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Further discussion:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"}> Gonzalo Reyes
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Typical and generic in a Baire space for relations
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Abraham+Robinson"}> Abraham Robinson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Infinite forcing in model theory
            %li
              %p
                Jon Barwise ed.
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Handbook of mathematical logic
            %li#Schoenfield71
              %p
                J. R. Schoenfield,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Unramified forcing
                %strong 13
                (1971) ((
                %a{:href => "https://www.math.ucsd.edu/~sbuss/CourseWeb/Math260_2012F2013W/Shoenfield_UnramifiedForcing.pdf"}> pdf
                ))
            %li
              %p
                S. Shelah,
                %em Proper and Improper Forcing
                , Perspectives in Math. Logic vol. 5 Springer Heidelberg 1998. (
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.pl/1235419814#toc"}> toc
                )
            %li
              %p
                MathOverflow:
                %em What is the generic poset used in forcing really?
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/q/51187/381"}> MO:q/51187
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#ReferencesInTermsOfClassifyingToposes In terms of classifying toposes
          %p
            On the interpretation of forcing via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+toposes"} classifying toposes
            (see also at
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kripke-Joyal+semantics"} Kripke-Joyal semantics
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
                , p. 5 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantifiers and sheaves
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/6630/846a00261a071b71e264e0f532e29cd9152f.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Myles+Tierney"}> Myles Tierney
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Forcing Topologies and Classifying Topoi
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Heller"} Alex Heller
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Myles+Tierney"} Myles Tierney
                (eds.)
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebra, Topology, and Category Theory: A Collection of Papers in Honor of Samuel Eilenberg
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/C2013-0-10841-0"} doi:10.1016/C2013-0-10841-0
            %li#MakkaiReyes77
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Makkai"}> Michael Makkai
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"} Gonzalo Reyes
                = succeed "," do
                  %em First Order Categorical Logic
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0066201"} doi:10.1007/BFb0066201
            %li#Johnstone77
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                , pages 270, 273, 348, 353 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Topos theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 10
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Blass"}> Andreas Blass
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrej+%C5%A0%C4%8Dedrov"} Andrej Ščedrov
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Classifying topoi and finite forcing
                %strong 28
                (1983) 111-140 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-4049(83)90085-3"}> doi:10.1016/0022-4049(83)90085-3
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Scedrov84
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrej+%C5%A0%C4%8Dedrov"}> Andrej Ščedrov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Forcing and classifying topoi
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/memo-48-295/5"} AMS:memo-48-295
            %li
              %p
                J. Benavides,
                %em The logic of sheaves, sheaf forcing and the independence of the Continuum Hypothesis
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1111.5854"}> arXiv:1111.5854
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Roberts15
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Roberts"}> David Roberts
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Class forcing and topos theory
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://indico.math.cnrs.fr/event/747/"} Topos at l’IHES
                (2015) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4225/55/5b2252e3092af"}> talk notes
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/4AaSySq8-GQ"} video recording
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Gratzer"}> Daniel Gratzer
                \:
                %em On The Independence of the Continuum Hypothesis
                (2019) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.danielgratzer.com/papers/on-the-independence.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Gratzer-IndependenceOfCH.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              %blockquote
                %p
                  In these notes we present a short, mostly self-contained proof of the independence of the
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+hypothesis"} continuum hypothesis
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos-theoretic
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
                  which can model
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
                  for which the continuum hypothesis fails. […] With these notes I hope to illustrate the elegant
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theoretic
                  approach to
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forcing"} forcing
                  results that has become increasingly common in logic,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %li#Shulman20
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
                \:
                %em Answer to ‘A better way to explain forcing?’
                (2020) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/a/370135"}> MO:a/370135
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            On
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Scedrov84"} Scedrov 84
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"} Peter Johnstone
            writes in his review (
            %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/2274338"}> The Journal of Symbolic Logic Vol. 50, No. 3 (Sep., 1985) pp 852-85
            ):
          %blockquote
            %p
              Until recently, logicians could have been forgiven for treating topos theory as a closed book, whose applications to logic (however interesting they might appear from the outside) were accessible only to insiders well schooled in the mysteries of category theory [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi …
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ldots
              ]. [Ščedrov’s book makes] a very clear case for the advantages of the topos-theoretic approach to forcing namely, that it provides a clear conceptual view of what generic structures really are, as well as a workable mean [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi …
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ldots
              ]. Although categorical ideas and terminology are inevitably present, they are handled so skillfully that the categoriphobic reader need have few qualms about them [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi …
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ldots
              ]. Ščedrov’s work ought to go a long way towards demolishing the wall of incomprehension that has hitherto prevented many logicians from appreciating what topos theory has to offer their subject.
